  <layout>One column of italic Latin verse, centred; below it at right a catchword "Latio-". No running head, no page number, no margin notes.</layout>
  <main_text>
    <p>Fluentis instar medius aureo templum</p>
    <p>Sophiaeque doctos imbuit lares fluxu</p>
    <p>Iam ripa inaudax semiliberumque intra</p>
    <p>Spatiatus alveum, nec fragosus incedens</p>
    <p>Nimiusve rerum, iam per aggerem praeceps</p>
    <p>In obvias se plebis inferens aures</p>
    <p>Torrentiorem, &amp; incitos simul fastus,</p>
    <p>Apicesque honoris &amp; superbia colles</p>
    <p>Altasque scelerum criminumque radices</p>
    <p>Fundo revellens; saxaque ipsa facunda</p>
    <p>Convulsa strage tabito emovens limo,</p>
    <p>Placide verendus: plurimum tamen lene</p>
    <p>Admurmurantis more rivuli labens,</p>
    <p>Propter sonori marginem cavum tophi,</p>
    <p>Quem creber ambiat sititor, illimem</p>
    <p>Vado videri, &amp; roscidum &amp; suum semper,</p>
    <p>Animisque titillante cereis flecti,</p>
    <p>Et gratioso blandus incidens sensu,</p>
    <p>Prae quam fluentis pura guttula electri,</p>
    <p>Tenerrimoque vulnerans sono pectus,</p>
    <p>Jecurque circum ludibundus &amp; fibris</p>
    <p>Penitus inerrans. Palpitare tunc castis</p>
    <p>Corda entheata gaudiis; decentesque</p>
    <p>Ridere fletus, prae dolore, sed lato</p>
    <p>Singultientes, flere risus, at motu</p>
    <p>Voluptuoso, quando melleus fandi</p>
    <p>Danaëio se pondere in sinus ipsos</p>
    <p>Penetrat imber, atque amabilem plenis</p>
    <p>Si qua est animula miselia, vagula, sol</p>
    <p>Rorem propinat poculis. Simul dulcis</p>
    <p>Siticulosa flore musteo Suadae</p>
    <p>Inebriatur auris, &amp; simul sancto</p>
    <p>Lymphatur aestu, seculique despectum</p>
    <p>Mens &amp; suave tedium imbibit vitae,</p>
    <p>Ardetque superas jugiter perennantes</p>
    <p>Non eloquendi fluminis cataractas,</p>
    <p>De fonte caelum purius refundentes,</p>
    <p>Lautamque vivis amphoris sitim stinguit</p>
    <p>Sub vorticosi mersa Numinis lymphis.</p>
    <p>Verende WITSI, cujus insidens labris</p>
    <p>Divina Peitho sacro sancta stillantem</p>
    <p>Latio-</p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>The last line "Latio-" stands at the lower right as a catchword. Some words are doubtful in the scan (e.g. "Peitho", "tophi", "electri", "miselia"); the faint marks at the page edges are specks, not text.</notes>
</page>
